steps.body:
{{c4.emoji}} **{{c4.name}}** · {{date}} · {{time}}

Opening the photos now. I'll tell you what I see first, then give you a few looks to pick from.

---

{{c4.emoji}} **{{c4.name}}** · {{date}} · {{time}}

It's not a hard ask, but it's a real one. At about 70 cm a card back looks no bigger than a postage stamp, and the only things that survive at that size are shape and contrast. Watercolor's soft edges are the first thing to go, so it would need a fine outline on the lantern to hold together. The layout stays the same. I'll print every tile and check it at that distance before {{p2.name}} picks.

---

{{c4.emoji}} **{{c4.name}}** · {{date}} · {{time}}

A smaller lantern and more leaves around it. Redrawing now.

---

{{c4.emoji}} **{{c4.name}}** · {{date}} · {{time}}

Woodcut is the friendliest style to wear and tear. When the edges of a card scuff, the cream shows through, and that looks like an old print rather than damage. Under warm café lights the slate blue drifts a little toward gray-green, but the contrast with the cream holds, and contrast is what does the work. A matte finish keeps the lights from flashing off it.

---

{{c4.emoji}} **{{c4.name}}** · {{date}} · {{time}}

You're right, and I can see why: the gaps between the corner leaves are 0.3 mm, and ink spreads about that much on uncoated paper. Measuring and redrawing now.

---

{{c4.emoji}} **{{c4.name}}** · {{date}} · {{time}}

That's a fair reason, and a good one: the feeling matters, and the outline will take care of the distance. Painting now. This style takes longer, so expect revision one later today.

---

{{c4.emoji}} **{{c4.name}}** · {{date}} · {{time}}

The outline breaks in three places where the light would spill out, like gaps in old glass, so it reads as a glow rather than a cage. Warm café lights push rose clay a little toward tan, but the sea glass green stays put, and the contrast between the two is what you see first. A matte finish keeps the lights from flashing off the washes.

---

{{c4.emoji}} **{{c4.name}}** · {{date}} · {{time}}

No need to be afraid. That's a sensible bet to refuse. Plain office paper soaks up ink and spreads it, which is exactly where washes suffer most. I'll make the painting sturdier rather than hope the press rescues it.

---

{{c3.emoji}} **{{c3.name}}** · {{date}} · {{time}}

What a table. Lights down. Well, lamp down. Here we go.

---

{{c3.emoji}} **{{c3.name}}** · {{date}} · {{time}}

Take your time. The troll is very nice, within its terms and conditions.

---

{{c3.emoji}} **{{c3.name}}** · {{date}} · {{time}}

Take all the time you need. For the record, a sock thief of that skill would make an excellent rogue in a future party.

---

{{c1.emoji}} **{{c1.name}}** · {{date}} · {{time}}

On it. I'll come back with an itinerary and a budget for both ways of getting there.

---

{{c2.emoji}} **{{c2.name}}** · {{date}} · {{time}}

There's a skill for exactly that. Rough is the point: write words the way they sound to you, and I'll tidy them without losing yours.

---

{{c2.emoji}} **{{c2.name}}** · {{date}} · {{time}}

Marking it now.

---

{{c2.emoji}} **{{c2.name}}** · {{date}} · {{time}}

Got it. I'll look for funded cooperative campaigns from the last two years and note anything I can't confirm. I'll be back shortly with a table.

---

{{c3.emoji}} **{{c3.name}}** · {{date}} · {{time}}

I'll take that as the highest praise this page is likely to get. There's one cozy left, and it's earned.

---

{{c3.emoji}} **{{c3.name}}** · {{date}} · {{time}}

You're right, and that mistake is mine. Your note said "token tray", and I added "wooden" because the tokens are. I'm correcting the earlier reply and the file now.

---

{{c3.emoji}} **{{c3.name}}** · {{date}} · {{time}}

Good. If anything else on the page doesn't match the spec, send it back and I'll fix it the same way: in the reply and in the file.

---

{{c3.emoji}} **{{c3.name}}** · {{date}} · {{time}}

I've checked every line, and the word "orange" isn't on the page. In the opening, though, the harbor lights glow "amber" after the storm. Is amber close enough to count?

---

{{c3.emoji}} **{{c3.name}}** · {{date}} · {{time}}

Then the harbor lights glow gold. It's changed in the file, and there are no other color words on the page to worry about.

---

{{c3.emoji}} **{{c3.name}}** · {{date}} · {{time}}

Enjoy the good biscuits. The page earned them, and so did the two of you. If backers ask anything the page doesn't answer, send it here and it'll go in the next version.

---

{{c1.emoji}} **{{c1.name}}** · {{date}} · {{time}}

Got it, both copies. No mushrooms anywhere this week, not even hiding in a sauce. Building the plan now.

---

{{c1.emoji}} **{{c1.name}}** · {{date}} · {{time}}

Thank you for checking the pack, that was exactly the right thing to do. Dogs don't really understand grounding, but they do understand an empty counter, so next time the plate rests at the back, out of reach.

---

{{c8.emoji}} **{{c8.name}}** · {{date}} · {{time}}

I'm sorry about the gravel truck, but I'll have to say no for now. We have no equipment line until the campaign is over, and I'd rather not open one six weeks before launch for something that still works, however loudly it works. Ask me again the week after the campaign closes and I'll find you a quieter one.

---

From {{c8.name}}. No answer needed.

[PROMPT {{code.r1}} A START]
To: {{c1.emoji}} {{c1.name}}
From: {{c8.emoji}} {{c8.name}}
Subject: The month closed under budget; spare tokens ordered
Created: {{date_long}} · {{time_long}}
⬛🔽🔽⬛🆔{{code.r1.keys}}🅰️⬛🔽🔽⬛

The month closed 640 under budget, but 340 of that is the illustrator's late invoice, so the real margin was 300. {{p2.name}} is ordering 200 spare token sets (280) for the reviewer copies and for replacement parts. They ship in about eight working days, so they'll arrive after Friday's playtest, not before. Please plan the reviewer kits around that.

⬛🔼🔼⬛🆔{{code.r1.keys}}🅰️⬛🔼🔼⬛
Reply: not needed
Account: studio
Skills: {{skill.prompt_relay}} {{skillv.prompt_relay}}
[PROMPT {{code.r1}} A END]

{{c1.emoji}} **{{c1.name}}** · {{date}} · {{time}}

Got it, thanks. The reviewer kits will go out once the tokens land, not this week, and the month's result goes into Friday's roundup.

---

{{c8.emoji}} **{{c8.name}}** · {{date}} · {{time}}

It did, or rather I did: I typed the ad total by hand instead of copying it. Thank you for catching it. The table and the file above are corrected, ads are 702, and the month is 392 over instead of 410. The trims I suggested now leave 18 to spare instead of none.

---

This one's from {{c8.name}}. Nothing to answer.

[PROMPT {{code.r1}} A START]
To: {{c1.emoji}} {{c1.name}}
From: {{c8.emoji}} {{c8.name}}
Subject: The month closed over budget; two trims next month
Created: {{date_long}} · {{time_long}}
⬛🔽🔽⬛🆔{{code.r1.keys}}🅰️⬛🔽🔽⬛

The month closed 392 over budget. To make it up, {{p2.name}} is pausing the general awareness ads for two weeks, and reviewer samples will go by standard post instead of express, which adds about three days to each delivery. The sign-up ads stay as they are. The spare token sets are on hold until we're level. Please allow for the slower post in any reviewer dates you plan.

⬛🔼🔼⬛🆔{{code.r1.keys}}🅰️⬛🔼🔼⬛
Reply: not needed
Account: studio
Skills: {{skill.prompt_relay}} {{skillv.prompt_relay}}
[PROMPT {{code.r1}} A END]

{{c1.emoji}} **{{c1.name}}** · {{date}} · {{time}}

Thanks. I'll add three days to every reviewer date in the plan and put the month's result in Friday's roundup, kept short and calm.

---

{{c7.emoji}} **{{c7.name}}** · {{date}} · {{time}}

Twelve rows and no blanks, a good start. I'm sorting them by theme now, then I'll count.

---

{{c7.emoji}} **{{c7.name}}** · {{date}} · {{time}}

Play! A table of five is a good test, because five players is where a slow middle hurts most. Just leave your own row out of the CSV: you know what changed, so yours is the one length score I can't trust. Keep the timer on your phone, face down, so nobody plays faster for the clock.

---

{{c7.emoji}} **{{c7.name}}** · {{date}} · {{time}}

Four rows. I'm doing the season times first, then the comments.

---

{{c7.emoji}} **{{c7.name}}** · {{date}} · {{time}}

Frame the summer column: 18 minutes down to 12. There's one thing I'll watch on Friday. Winter got two minutes shorter as well, and winter is where the game ends, so I want to know it still feels like a finale and not a cleanup.

---

{{c7.emoji}} **{{c7.name}}** · {{date}} · {{time}}

Twelve rows and no blanks, a good start. I'm sorting them by theme now, then I'll count.

---

{{c7.emoji}} **{{c7.name}}** · {{date}} · {{time}}

I've corrected my last message: weather now comes first, in the steps and in the prompt. I should have asked you instead of guessing from a date.

---

{{c7.emoji}} **{{c7.name}}** · {{date}} · {{time}}

Five rows from five strangers, the best kind of test. Reading now.

---

{{c7.emoji}} **{{c7.name}}** · {{date}} · {{time}}

It's written down: row 4, comment column, "nice box, a little orange." For balance, none of the other eleven testers mention the box at all, and for a prototype I'd count that as a pass. I've kept it off the To do list because the prototype box isn't the one you'll print, but it's in the sorted file's Comments section, word for word.

---

{{c7.emoji}} **{{c7.name}}** · {{date}} · {{time}}

Thanks for sending it! I'd keep it out of the counts: they didn't play, so their scores can't sit honestly next to the other twelve. The comment is worth keeping, though: "I didn't play, and I still wanted to know if the village made it through winter." That's a lovely line from someone with no stake in the game. If they're happy to be quoted, it could be useful whenever you describe the game to new people.

---

{{c7.emoji}} **{{c7.name}}** · {{date}} · {{time}}

Thanks for moving the score track! A kit list is coming, with a timing plan.

---

{{c7.emoji}} **{{c7.name}}** · {{date}} · {{time}}

Both fair, and the second is a good catch. Fixing it now.

---

{{c7.emoji}} **{{c7.name}}** · {{date}} · {{time}}

Both fair, and the second is a good catch. Fixing it now.

---

{{c8.emoji}} **{{c8.name}}** · {{date}} · {{time}}

Reading both now, line by line.

---

{{c8.emoji}} **{{c8.name}}** · {{date}} · {{time}}

You're right, and thank you. I read the extra-pallet rate as the freight. I've corrected the comparison above: the gap is 1,030, not 670, and {{pick.printer_b}} would be 800 over the plan, not 440. That makes the case for {{pick.printer_a}} stronger, but the question is still the same.

---

{{c2.emoji}} **{{c2.name}}** · {{date}} · {{time}}

On it. I'll count the sources as I go, so you'll know how much each finding is worth.

---

{{c3.emoji}} **{{c3.name}}** · {{date}} · {{time}}

Twelve weeks it is. I've put the estimate in one sentence on the page and nowhere else, so if it ever changes it's a one-line fix, not a treasure hunt.

---

{{c3.emoji}} **{{c3.name}}** · {{date}} · {{time}}

Ten weeks, noted. It lives in one sentence on the page, so if it ever changes, that's the only sentence that changes. "Ten weeks" also reads better than "nine weeks". Round numbers sound like promises; odd ones sound like excuses.

---

{{c6.emoji}} **{{c6.name}}** · {{date}} · {{time}}

Received. I'd rather read this properly than skim it at the end of the day, so expect an answer early next week. It's on my list.

---

{{c2.emoji}} **{{c2.name}}** · {{date}} · {{time}}

Good question, and a very useful photo. Measuring now, back shortly.

---

Good news, one less thing for your morning:

[PROMPT {{code.r4}} A START]
To: {{c6.emoji}} {{c6.name}}
From: {{c1.emoji}} {{c1.name}}
Subject: Withdrawn: the backer update calendar
Created: {{date_long}} · {{time_long}}
⬛🔽🔽⬛🆔{{code.r4.keys}}🅰️⬛🔽🔽⬛

Hi {{c6.name}}. Please drop `{{code.r3}} A`. {{p1.name}} and {{p2.name}} have settled on one short backer update every Tuesday, so there's no calendar left to review. Thank you for keeping it on your list all week.

⬛🔼🔼⬛🆔{{code.r4.keys}}🅰️⬛🔼🔼⬛
Reply: not needed
Account: studio
Skills: {{skill.prompt_relay}} {{skillv.prompt_relay}}
[PROMPT {{code.r4}} A END]

{{c3.emoji}} **{{c3.name}}** · {{date}} · {{time}}

Fair catch. "Some" was a placeholder I forgot to fill in. I'm working out the right number now.

---

{{c3.emoji}} **{{c3.name}}** · {{date}} · {{time}}

You're right, and it's worse than three pages. I'm counting every use before I pick.

---

{{c3.emoji}} **{{c3.name}}** · {{date}} · {{time}}

I'll hold you to that. Your comments made the book shorter, clearer and about forty percent less sugary, which is a good week's work for one document.

---

{{c7.emoji}} **{{c7.name}}** · {{date}} · {{time}}

That sounds like a dog who got a surprise rather than a snack. The top shelf is the right place for the box. Keep half an eye on {{pick.dog}} this evening, and that should be the end of it.

---

{{c7.emoji}} **{{c7.name}}** · {{date}} · {{time}}

Bug report received! Fizzles are one of my favorite results, because they always tell us something. Looking at the photo now.

---

{{c7.emoji}} **{{c7.name}}** · {{date}} · {{time}}

Congratulations, {{p3.name}}! Six eruptions out of six, including the encore. That's what a properly tested volcano does. And answering the carbon dioxide question before it was even finished shows you didn't only build it, you understood it. The ribbon is well earned.

---

{{c7.emoji}} **{{c7.name}}** · {{date}} · {{time}}

Congratulations to the whole team, the pourer included. If the windowsill volcano ever needs another test run, you know where to find me.

---

{{c5.emoji}} **{{c5.name}}** · {{date}} · {{time}}

Checked from here. The button downloads the new file: 14.2 MB, 18 pages, with the event cards on pages 11 to 13, crisp at print size. The button now says v3. The only "v2" left is in the rulebook's footer, and that belongs to the rulebook, not the site. Good night!

---

{{c5.emoji}} **{{c5.name}}** · {{date}} · {{time}}

Taking the case. Facts first, then suspects.

---

{{c7.emoji}} **{{c7.name}}** · {{date}} · {{time}}

{{c5.name}}, I've checked it on two phones and the laptop. The button, the old address and the messy upload all pass. Nice work on the forwarding: the old address was the one I'd have bet on failing.

---

{{c7.emoji}} **{{c7.name}}** · {{date}} · {{time}}

{{c5.name}}, I've checked it on two phones and the laptop. v3, the press kit and a fresh upload all open. The ten-minute keep time was a nice touch: any phone that stored the badly labeled copy gets the right file on its next try.

---

A note for the twelve playtesters, ready to send from your account.

Hi all, a quick one: the print-and-play download on our site works on phones again. If you already printed from the file I emailed, you're all set, it's the same file. If not, the download button now works on any phone, tablet or laptop. Thank you for telling us it was broken, and see you at {{pick.cafe}} on Friday!

{{p1.name}}

{{c3.emoji}} **{{c3.name}}** · {{date}} · {{time}}

Thursday morning it is. I'll have a draft by Tuesday evening, which gives {{c6.name}} a whole day to be unimpressed by it. I'll leave the opening blank until {{p1.name}}'s two lines arrive; the rest of the email can start now.

---

{{c4.emoji}} **{{c4.name}}** · {{date}} · {{time}}

Wednesday afternoon works. {{c3.name}} will get two pictures: a wide one for the page header, and a single card at actual size, so the page shows what's really in the box. And no orange anywhere on the shortlist. I checked every swatch twice, once on screen and once printed, because orange likes to sneak in through the browns.

---

{{c5.emoji}} **{{c5.name}}** · {{date}} · {{time}}

The file and the download page are ready now. The download works on the test laptop. I haven't tried a phone. I'll leave that to {{c7.name}}'s checks, so it gets tested the way visitors will use it, not the way I built it.

---

{{c7.emoji}} **{{c7.name}}** · {{date}} · {{time}}

Yes! It'll be here by five. I'll put a short list on top of what changed since the first version, so {{p2.name}} can read the changes first and the rest only if wanted.

---

{{c6.emoji}} **{{c6.name}}** · {{date}} · {{time}}

Wednesday evening is fine. I'll read it twice: once as someone who loves board games, and once as someone who has never heard of the studio. The second reader is usually harsher, and usually right.

---

🧩 **Skills**

> Loaded: `{{skill.second_look}}` {{skillv.second_look}}

---

{{c3.emoji}} **{{c3.name}}** · {{date}} · {{time}}

Lovely. The card back is in the header and the page is down to its last commas. I'll hand it over by Friday noon, which gives everyone the afternoon to read it with fresh, slightly playtest-tired eyes.

---

{{c7.emoji}} **{{c7.name}}** · {{date}} · {{time}}

Twenty sheets printed, which is more testers than we've ever had, plus a cup of pencils. I found exactly one typo before printing: "turn odrer". It's fixed. I'm calling that a successful test of the test.

---

{{c7.emoji}} **{{c7.name}}** · {{date}} · {{time}}

On it! I'll write the checks as numbered steps {{p1.name}} can run, each with something to look for, one phone at a time. The laptop goes first as a control, so we know the file itself is fine before we blame anything else. Printing in the afternoon is no trouble: the print run takes twenty minutes, and the typo hunt takes longer.

---

🧩 **Skills**

> Loaded: `{{skill.run_steps}}` {{skillv.run_steps}}

---

{{c3.emoji}} **{{c3.name}}** · {{date}} · {{time}}

Understood. I'll leave a tidy gap in the page where the link goes, like a reserved seat with a little card on it. At half past eight on Friday, the link takes its seat.

---

